-- Arena challenges batch 3/14: rows 051-075 (track: Communication Systems, category: ECE)

Formula: m = A_m / A_c','{"choices": ["2.5", "0.25", "0.4", "40%"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'m = A_m / A_c = 4/10 = 0.4 (or 40%). Modulation index m ≤ 1 for undistorted AM. When m > 1, overmodulation causes severe distortion.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.85) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

BW ≈ 2(Δf + f_m)','{"choices": ["75 kHz", "150 kHz", "180 kHz", "90 kHz"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'BW = 2(Δf + f_m) = 2(75 + 15) = 2 × 90 = 180 kHz. This is the standard FM broadcast channel bandwidth.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.82) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

Nyquist criterion: f_s ≥ 2 × f_max','{"choices": ["2 kHz", "8 kHz", "4 kHz", "16 kHz"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'f_s_min = 2 × f_max = 2 × 4 kHz = 8 kHz. Telephony (voice) is sampled at 8 kHz (Nyquist for 4 kHz bandwidth), CD audio at 44.1 kHz (Nyquist for ~22 kHz).','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.88) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

Bit rate = f_s × bits/sample','{"choices": ["8 kbps", "64 kbps", "16 kbps", "128 kbps"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'Bit rate = 8000 × 8 = 64,000 bps = 64 kbps. This is the standard G.711 PCM voice coding rate used in telephony (PSTN, VoIP).','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.87) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

SNR_dB = 10 log(P_signal / P_noise)','{"choices": ["20 dB", "10 dB", "30 dB", "100 dB"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'SNR = 10 log(100/0.1) = 10 log(1000) = 30 dB. Every 3 dB represents doubling of SNR; 30 dB = 1000× power ratio.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.83) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

Bits per symbol = log₂(M) where M = number of states.','{"choices": ["4 bits", "1 bit", "3 bits", "2 bits"], "correct": 3}'::jsonb,'Bits per symbol = log₂(4) = 2 bits. QPSK is bandwidth-efficient: each symbol carries 2 bits. 16-QAM carries 4 bits/symbol; 64-QAM carries 6 bits/symbol.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.85) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

Bits per symbol = log₂(16) = 4. Bit rate = Symbol rate × bits/symbol.','{"choices": ["10 Mbps", "160 Mbps", "40 Mbps", "2.5 Mbps"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'Bit rate = 10×10⁶ × 4 = 40 Mbps. This is why modern modems use high-order QAM (256-QAM, 1024-QAM): to achieve high bit rates within a fixed bandwidth.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.78) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

C = B × log₂(1 + SNR)','{"choices": ["200 kbps", "1 Mbps", "1.6 Mbps", "800 kbps"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'C = 200×10³ × log₂(1+31) = 200×10³ × log₂(32) = 200×10³ × 5 = 1,000,000 bps = 1 Mbps. Shannon capacity is the theoretical maximum for the channel.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.72) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

FSPL(dB) ≈ 20log(d) + 20log(f) + 20log(4π/c) ≈ 20log(d) + 20log(f) − 147.6','{"choices": ["40 dB", "60 dB", "80 dB", "20 dB"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'FSPL = 20log(100) + 20log(2.4×10⁹) − 147.6 = 40 + 187.6 − 147.6 = 80 dB. Path loss increases with distance squared and frequency squared.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.68) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

PG = Chip rate / Data rate (or BW_spread / BW_signal)','{"choices": ["9.6", "128", "256", "64"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'PG = 1,228,800 / 9,600 = 128 = 21 dB. The spreading gain allows multiple users to share the same spectrum; each user''s signal appears as wideband noise to others.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.65) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

P_total = P_c(1 + m²/2)','{"choices": ["11.6 kW", "10 kW", "13.2 kW", "12 kW"], "correct": 2}'::jsonb,'P_total = 10000 × (1 + 0.64/2) = 10000 × 1.32 = 13.2 kW. At m=1, sideband power = P_c/2. The carrier wastes 10 kW without carrying information — SSB eliminates this inefficiency.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.67) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

P_r = P_t × G_T × G_R × (λ/4πd)²','{"choices": ["0.1 µW", "12.6 nW", "−50 dBW", "1.58 nW"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'λ = c/f = 0.3 m; (λ/4πd)² = (0.3/4π×1000)² = (2.387×10⁻⁵)² = 5.7×10⁻¹⁰; G_T=10, G_R=3.16; P_r = 10×10×3.16×5.7×10⁻¹⁰ ≈ 12.6 nW.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.58) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

BER = Q(√(2E_b/N_0))

At E_b/N_0 = 8 dB ≈ 6.3, what is the approximate BER?','{"choices": ["10⁻¹", "10⁻³", "10⁻⁵", "10⁻⁷"], "correct": 1}'::jsonb,'At E_b/N_0 = 8 dB: Q(√(12.6)) = Q(3.55) ≈ 2×10⁻⁴. The BPSK BER waterfall curve drops steeply around 5–10 dB. Practical systems target BER < 10⁻⁶ before FEC.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.5) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

G = η × (πD/λ)²','{"choices": ["39.8 dBi", "100", "1000", "30 dBi"], "correct": 0}'::jsonb,'G = 0.6 × (π×1/0.03)² = 0.6 × (104.7)² = 0.6 × 10971 = 6583 = 38.2 dBi ≈ 39.8 dBi. Parabolic dishes provide high gain for point-to-point microwave and satellite links.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.58) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

C/N = EIRP − FSPL + G_r − N_system − BW','{"choices": ["Yes, margin = 4.4 dB", "No, margin = −4.4 dB", "Yes, margin = 24.4 dB", "Yes, margin = 0 dB"], "correct": 0}'::jsonb,'C = 50 − 200 + 40 = −110 dBW; N = −160 + 75.6 = −84.4 dBW; C/N = −110 − (−84.4) = −25.6 dB... The exact result depends on precise noise temperature. The concept: C/N must exceed the threshold with positive margin for reliable link.','{multiple_choice}'::text[],0.45) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
